{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789900496,"nonce":"osVlEPzJl18pjWc1yy9j/rFEV4s=","title":"Each LLVM promotion compiles one function in a module of its own: ten hot functions take 60 ms of sequential compiles","message":"richards, NUC, ZYNTAX_OSR_TRACE=1 with timestamps: the ten loop-free methods become hot together (entry probes, ~93 ms) and are promoted one after another on the promotion broker thread, each taking about 6 ms (3 ms of IR generation and optimisation to the \"llvm install\" line, 3 ms of MCJIT engine creation and codegen after it) for a function of 20 to 150 instructions. The LLVM tier is complete only at ~135 ms, by which time a 50-iteration run is over; iterations 5 to 40 run on Cranelift at 1.2 ms instead of 0.75, and the speed-center mean (over 50 iterations) reads 1.0 to 1.1 ms against a steady state of 0.75.\n\nBatching the promotions that are hot together into one LLVM module would pay the engine and module set-up once, and the functions could inline each other; a worker per core on the broker would overlap the compiles. Either would move the LLVM tier to within the first ten iterations.","files":null}]}